/**
  @page guides-polytopes Polytopes
  @brief Reference shapes, physical cells and their lower-dimensional entities.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section guides-polytopes-intro Cells and their geometry

  A mesh combines vertices, edges, faces and cells through incidence relations.
  The cells have the mesh's topological dimension; their coordinates lie in
  the embedding space. A triangular surface mesh therefore has dimension two
  even when its vertices have three coordinates.

  `Polytope::Type` identifies a reference shape. A concrete
  @ref Rodin::Geometry::Polytope "Polytope" also identifies an entity in a mesh,
  with its connectivity, attribute and geometric transformation.

  @section guides-polytopes-dimensions Dimension and role

  | Dimension | Entities | Role |
  | :--- | :--- | :--- |
  | 0 | Points | Vertices |
  | 1 | Segments | Edges; cells of a curve mesh |
  | 2 | Triangles and quadrilaterals | Faces; cells of a surface mesh |
  | 3 | Tetrahedra, pyramids, hexahedra and wedges | Volume cells |

  A **facet** is an entity of dimension one less than a cell. Facets are edges
  in a two-dimensional mesh and faces in a three-dimensional mesh.

  @section guides-polytopes-types Supported reference shapes

  | `Polytope::Type` | Dimension | Vertices | Reference construction |
  | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
  | `Point` | 0 | 1 | A point |
  | `Segment` | 1 | 2 | Unit interval |
  | `Triangle` | 2 | 3 | Unit simplex |
  | `Quadrilateral` | 2 | 4 | Product of two unit intervals |
  | `Tetrahedron` | 3 | 4 | Unit simplex |
  | `Pyramid` | 3 | 5 | Collapsed square cross-sections |
  | `Hexahedron` | 3 | 8 | Product of three unit intervals |
  | `Wedge` | 3 | 6 | Triangle times unit interval |

  The figures and tables use the reference vertices returned by
  `Polytope::Traits(type).getVertex(i)`. The numbered markers identify the
  zero-based local vertex indices.

  @m_class{rodin-mesh-stages}

  @parblock
  @image html ReferencePolytopes-point.png "Point reference vertex."
  @image html ReferencePolytopes-segment.png "Segment reference vertices."
  @image html ReferencePolytopes-triangle.png "Triangle reference vertices."
  @image html ReferencePolytopes-quadrilateral.png "Quadrilateral reference vertices."
  @image html ReferencePolytopes-tetrahedron.png "Tetrahedron reference vertices."
  @image html ReferencePolytopes-pyramid.png "Pyramid reference vertices."
  @image html ReferencePolytopes-hexahedron.png "Hexahedron reference vertices."
  @image html ReferencePolytopes-wedge.png "Wedge reference vertices."
  @endparblock

  @section guides-polytopes-vertices Reference vertices and local ordering

  The tables give each reference vertex @f$\widehat v_i@f$ in local order.
  Coordinate tuples have the reference cell's topological dimension.

  For a physical cell @f$K@f$ with local-to-global vertex connectivity
  @f$(I_0,\ldots,I_{n-1})@f$, its geometric map satisfies
  @f$F_K(\widehat v_i)=x_{I_i}@f$. The local index @f$i@f$ selects a position
  in that cell's connectivity; @f$I_i@f$ identifies the vertex in the mesh.
  Preserve this order when constructing connectivity or supplying geometric
  vertex data.

  @subsection guides-polytopes-vertices-point Point

  The reference point is the unique element of @f$\mathbb R^0@f$. Its
  coordinate vector is empty, written @f$()@f$, and `getVertex(0).size()` is zero.

  | Local index | Reference coordinates |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | 0 | @f$\widehat v_0=()@f$ |

  @subsection guides-polytopes-vertices-segment Segment

  | Local index | Reference coordinates |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | 0 | @f$\widehat v_0=(0)@f$ |
  | 1 | @f$\widehat v_1=(1)@f$ |

  @subsection guides-polytopes-vertices-triangle Triangle

  | Local index | Reference coordinates |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | 0 | @f$\widehat v_0=(0,0)@f$ |
  | 1 | @f$\widehat v_1=(1,0)@f$ |
  | 2 | @f$\widehat v_2=(0,1)@f$ |

  @subsection guides-polytopes-vertices-quadrilateral Quadrilateral

  | Local index | Reference coordinates |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | 0 | @f$\widehat v_0=(0,0)@f$ |
  | 1 | @f$\widehat v_1=(1,0)@f$ |
  | 2 | @f$\widehat v_2=(1,1)@f$ |
  | 3 | @f$\widehat v_3=(0,1)@f$ |

  @subsection guides-polytopes-vertices-tetrahedron Tetrahedron

  | Local index | Reference coordinates |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | 0 | @f$\widehat v_0=(0,0,0)@f$ |
  | 1 | @f$\widehat v_1=(1,0,0)@f$ |
  | 2 | @f$\widehat v_2=(0,1,0)@f$ |
  | 3 | @f$\widehat v_3=(0,0,1)@f$ |

  @subsection guides-polytopes-vertices-pyramid Pyramid

  Vertices zero through three form the square base. The apex is vertex four,
  directly above vertex zero. Horizontal cross-sections shrink toward that apex.

  | Local index | Reference coordinates |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | 0 | @f$\widehat v_0=(0,0,0)@f$ |
  | 1 | @f$\widehat v_1=(1,0,0)@f$ |
  | 2 | @f$\widehat v_2=(1,1,0)@f$ |
  | 3 | @f$\widehat v_3=(0,1,0)@f$ |
  | 4 | @f$\widehat v_4=(0,0,1)@f$ |

  @subsection guides-polytopes-vertices-hexahedron Hexahedron

  Vertices zero through three lie on the bottom face. Vertices four through
  seven are their corresponding vertices on the top face.

  | Local index | Reference coordinates |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | 0 | @f$\widehat v_0=(0,0,0)@f$ |
  | 1 | @f$\widehat v_1=(1,0,0)@f$ |
  | 2 | @f$\widehat v_2=(1,1,0)@f$ |
  | 3 | @f$\widehat v_3=(0,1,0)@f$ |
  | 4 | @f$\widehat v_4=(0,0,1)@f$ |
  | 5 | @f$\widehat v_5=(1,0,1)@f$ |
  | 6 | @f$\widehat v_6=(1,1,1)@f$ |
  | 7 | @f$\widehat v_7=(0,1,1)@f$ |

  @subsection guides-polytopes-vertices-wedge Wedge

  Vertices zero through two form the bottom triangle. Vertices three through
  five are their corresponding vertices on the top triangle.

  | Local index | Reference coordinates |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | 0 | @f$\widehat v_0=(0,0,0)@f$ |
  | 1 | @f$\widehat v_1=(1,0,0)@f$ |
  | 2 | @f$\widehat v_2=(0,1,0)@f$ |
  | 3 | @f$\widehat v_3=(0,0,1)@f$ |
  | 4 | @f$\widehat v_4=(1,0,1)@f$ |
  | 5 | @f$\widehat v_5=(0,1,1)@f$ |

  @section guides-polytopes-reference Reference and physical coordinates

  A geometric map @f$F_K:\widehat K\to K@f$ sends a reference point to a
  physical point. Reference coordinates determine basis values and reference
  quadrature samples; the map supplies physical coordinates and derivatives.
  On a curved cell, the map also depends on its geometric control points.

  Reference vertices can be obtained directly from the library.

  @code{.cpp}
  const Polytope::Traits traits(Polytope::Type::Pyramid);
  for (size_t i = 0; i < traits.getVertexCount(); ++i)
  {
    const auto& reference = traits.getVertex(i);
    // Use reference coordinates in the order defined by the element.
  }
  @endcode

  A `Geometry::Point` combines a cell with reference coordinates. Its Jacobian
  has embedding dimension rows and cell dimension columns. For surface meshes
  it is rectangular. The @ref guides-quadrature "quadrature guide" explains the
  corresponding measure factors in a complete checked example.

  @section guides-polytopes-meshgen Generate a mesh

  @ref examples-geometry-uniformgrid "Uniform grids" are available for supported
  volume-cell shapes. Axis counts specify vertices; coordinates initially have
  unit spacing. Selecting a reference shape and selecting the physical domain
  are separate choices. Scale the generated coordinates to obtain a unit domain.

  @section guides-polytopes-sub Lower-dimensional entities

  A triangle has three edges and three vertices; a tetrahedron has four faces,
  six edges and four vertices. The mesh stores these incidences through
  @ref Rodin::Geometry::Connectivity "Connectivity". Relations are computed
  on demand. For example, `compute(1, 2)` discovers edges and associates each
  edge with its incident cells in a triangular mesh.

  @subsection guides-polytopes-sub-local Inspect cell-induced ordering

  `getSubPolytopes(entities, cellIndex, d)` enumerates a cell's
  @f$d@f$-dimensional sub-entities in their local order. Each result contains
  a geometry type and an ordered vertex key. The key contains mesh vertex
  indices in the order induced by the parent cell.

  For a pyramid with cell vertex key @f$(I_0,I_1,I_2,I_3,I_4)@f$, the
  face sequence uses the following positions in that key.

  | Local face | Shape | Parent-cell vertex positions |
  | :--- | :--- | :--- |
  | 0 | Quadrilateral | @f$(0,1,2,3)@f$ |
  | 1 | Triangle | @f$(0,1,4)@f$ |
  | 2 | Triangle | @f$(1,2,4)@f$ |
  | 3 | Triangle | @f$(2,3,4)@f$ |
  | 4 | Triangle | @f$(3,0,4)@f$ |

  The edge sequence follows the base loop
  @f$(0,1),(1,2),(2,3),(3,0)@f$, followed by the apex edges
  @f$(0,4),(1,4),(2,4),(3,4)@f$.

  @subsection guides-polytopes-sub-connectivity Inspect stored connectivity

  `compute(D, d)` builds the cell-to-entity relation for a mesh of topological
  dimension @f$D@f$. `compute(d, D)` builds the reverse relation, listing the
  cells incident to each entity. `getIndex(d, key)` resolves a vertex key to
  the entity's mesh index, and `getPolytope(d, index)` exposes its stored
  vertex order.

  The following code inspects the edges and faces of cell zero in a small
  pyramid mesh. It uses `Rodin::Geometry`, `Rodin::Context`, `<iostream>` and
  `<vector>`.

  @code{.cpp}
  Mesh mesh = Mesh().UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Pyramid, {2, 2, 2});
  auto& connectivity = mesh.getConnectivity();
  const size_t D = mesh.getDimension();
  const Index cellIndex = 0;

  std::cout << "Cell vertices";
  for (Index vertex : connectivity.getPolytope(D, cellIndex))
    std::cout << ' ' << vertex;
  std::cout << '\n';

  for (size_t d : {size_t{1}, size_t{2}})
  {
    connectivity.compute(D, d);
    connectivity.compute(d, D);
    std::vector<Connectivity<Context::Local>::SubPolytope> entities;
    connectivity.getSubPolytopes(entities, cellIndex, d);

    for (size_t local = 0; local < entities.size(); ++local)
    {
      const auto& induced = entities[local].vertices;
      const auto index = connectivity.getIndex(d, induced);
      std::cout << "Dimension " << d << ", local " << local
                << ", mesh index " << *index << '\n';
      std::cout << "  Cell-induced vertices";
      for (Index vertex : induced)
        std::cout << ' ' << vertex;
      std::cout << "\n  Stored vertices";
      for (Index vertex : connectivity.getPolytope(d, *index))
        std::cout << ' ' << vertex;
      std::cout << "\n  Incident cells";
      for (Index cell : connectivity.getIncidence({d, D}, *index))
        std::cout << ' ' << cell;
      std::cout << '\n';
    }
  }
  @endcode

  This cell has eight edges and five faces. The reverse incidence lists expose
  which entities are shared with neighboring cells.

  The entries returned by `getIncidence({D, d}, cellIndex)` are mesh indices
  of the incident entities. Each index is resolved through
  `getPolytope(d, index)` when its vertex order is needed.

  A shared edge or face has one stored vertex order. An incident cell can
  induce a different order on that same entity. Comparing the two ordered
  keys gives the vertex permutation needed to align traces or local facet
  data. Oriented integration additionally requires the appropriate normal
  convention for each incident cell. The
  @ref examples-interface-traces "interface traces tutorial" demonstrates
  two-sided values on a shared edge.

  @section guides-polytopes-iteration Traverse mesh entities

  The @ref guides-meshes-iteration "iteration guide" describes the cell,
  vertex and facet iterators. Entity indices belong to their own dimension;
  edge index zero and cell index zero identify different objects.

  @section guides-polytopes-choosing Select a cell family

  Cell shape, geometric mapping, finite element family and polynomial degree
  jointly determine approximation properties. Triangles and tetrahedra are
  simplices; quadrilaterals and hexahedra support tensor-product constructions;
  wedges combine a triangular section with an interval. Pyramids connect
  quadrilateral and triangular faces and require their own reference basis.
  Accuracy comparisons should keep the problem, degrees of freedom, quadrature
  and mesh quality explicit.

  @section guides-polytopes-see-also Continue

  - @ref guides-meshes "Mesh organization"
  - @ref guides-connectivity "Incidence and boundary detection"
  - @ref fem-ciarlet "Finite element definition"
  - @ref guides-quadrature "Reference and mapped quadrature"
*/
